9. TRANSFER API
==================================================

Inspect whether the existing goal update API can safely support the transfer.

Prefer a dedicated transactional endpoint when it provides safer validation and auditability, for example:

POST /api/goals/:goalId/transfer

Example request:

{
  "targetTeamId": "target-team-id",
  "ownerId": "optional-owner-id",
  "goalLevelId": "optional-compatible-level-id",
  "reason": "Goal was assigned to the wrong team"
}

Do not treat these client values as trusted.

The server must:

1. Authenticate the user.
2. Load the goal.
3. Verify goal access.
4. Verify source-team permission.
5. Verify target-team permission.
6. Verify the target team exists and is active.
7. Verify the target team is different from the source team.
8. Verify owner eligibility in the target team.
9. Verify the goal level is allowed for the target team.
10. Validate relationships and dependent data.
11. Update the goal inside a safe transaction where supported.
12. Add an audit/history entry.
13. Return the updated goal with the current team and permission data.

Use the current project API pattern. Do not create an unnecessary endpoint if the existing architecture already has a safe transfer action.

